@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://www.w3.org/ns/ldt/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
  
<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "RDF imports" ;
    dct:description "RDF imports and transforming RDF using SPARQL CONSTRUCT" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .
    
<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="lead">RDF imports and transforming RDF using SPARQL CONSTRUCT</p>
    <p>An RDF import is a combination of multiple resources:</p>
    <dl>
        <dt>File</dt>
        <dd>The RDF file to be imported</dd>
        <dt>Transformation query (optional)</dt>
        <dd>A user-defined <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query that transforms the input RDF into another RDF</dd>
        <dt>Graph (optional)</dt>
        <dd>The target graph (document) to which converted items will be <a href="../../http-api/#ld-post">POST</a>ed</dd>
    </dl>
    <p><em>Either the transformation query or the target graph needs to be specified, not both.</em></p>
    <p>If the graph is specified, the resulting RDF data is appended to a single document.</p>
    <p>If the transformation query is specified, the resulting data should contain document instances in order to attach to the <a href="../../dataset/#structure">document hierarchy</a>. The documents have to be URI resources. The document graphs have to be <em>explicitly specified</em> using a <code>GRAPH</code> block in the <code>CONSTRUCT</code> template (which is a
    <a href="https://jena.apache.org/documentation/query/construct-quad.html" target="_blank">Jena-specific extension</a> of SPARQL 1.1), otherwise the import result will end up in the default graph of the application's RDF dataset, which is not accessible via LinkedDataHub.</p>
    <p>The import process runs asynchronously in the background, i.e. the import item is created before the process completes.
        Currently the only way to determine when it completes is to refresh the import item and check the import
        status (completed/failed). Upon successful report, metadata such as the number of imported RDF triples is attached
        to the import.</p>
    <p>The resulting RDF is split into documents (named graphs), which are then created one by one and validated against <a href="../../administration/ontologies/#constraints">constraints</a> in the process.
        Constraint violations, if any, are attached to the import item.</p>
    <div>
        <p>The running example connects the Northwind Traders dataspace to the <a href="https://unesco-thesaurus.demo.linkeddatahub.com/" target="_blank">UNESCO Thesaurus dataspace</a>:
            each Northwind product category is mapped to the thesaurus concept it corresponds to, using the SKOS mapping relations. The mapping file
            doubles the categories as <code>skos:Concept</code>s, which makes the relations applicable (and lets the
            <a href="../../administration/packages/" target="_blank">SKOS package</a> render the categories as concepts):</p>
        <pre>@prefix skos:   &lt;http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#&gt; .

# 1 Beverages -&gt; Beverages
&lt;https://northwind-traders.demo.linkeddatahub.com/categories/1/#this&gt; a skos:Concept ;
    skos:closeMatch &lt;https://unesco-thesaurus.demo.linkeddatahub.com/concepts/concept3683/#this&gt; .

# 2 Condiments -&gt; Food (no finer concept in the thesaurus)
&lt;https://northwind-traders.demo.linkeddatahub.com/categories/2/#this&gt; a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broadMatch &lt;https://unesco-thesaurus.demo.linkeddatahub.com/concepts/concept1812/#this&gt; .

# 3 Confections -&gt; Sugar
&lt;https://northwind-traders.demo.linkeddatahub.com/categories/3/#this&gt; a skos:Concept ;
    skos:relatedMatch &lt;https://unesco-thesaurus.demo.linkeddatahub.com/concepts/concept3495/#this&gt; .

# … one mapping per category, 8 in total</pre>
        <p class="exhibit-links">Source: <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub-Apps/blob/master/demo/northwind-traders/categories/unesco-mappings.ttl" target="_blank">categories/unesco-mappings.ttl</a></p>
        <p>Imported with the <em>graph</em> variant, the whole file is appended to one target document — a single graph of mappings that describe the
            two dataspaces' resources:</p>
        <pre>&lt;#mappings-import&gt; a ldh:RDFImport ;
    dct:title "UNESCO category mappings" ;
    ldh:file &lt;https://localhost:4443/uploads/…&gt; ;
    sd:name &lt;https://localhost:4443/categories/mappings/&gt; .</pre>
        <div>
            <h3 id="transformation">Transformation</h3>
            <p>This step is used to transform the RDF data that is being imported, if necessary (to a different vocabulary, for example). It also connects instances in the imported data to the documents in LinkedDataHub's <a href="../../dataset/" target="_blank">dataset</a>.</p>
            <p>The mapping is a user-defined SPARQL <code>CONSTRUCT</code> that follows the shared <a href="../#mapping-queries">mapping query rules</a>.</p>
            <div>
                <h4 id="transformation-example">Example</h4>
                <p>Imported with the <em>transformation</em> variant instead, each mapping is routed into its own category's document. The mapping file
                    describes the <em>demo's</em> categories, so the transformation also rebases their URIs onto this dataspace — an ETL step that makes the
                    same file installable against any base URI:</p>
                <pre><code>PREFIX skos:  &lt;http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#&gt;

CONSTRUCT
{
    GRAPH ?graph
    {
        ?category a skos:Concept ;
            ?mapping ?concept .
    }
}
WHERE
{
    ?remote a skos:Concept ;
        ?mapping ?concept .
    FILTER (?mapping IN (skos:closeMatch, skos:broadMatch, skos:relatedMatch))

    # rebase the demo's category URIs onto this dataspace's $base
    BIND (STRAFTER(STR(?remote), "northwind-traders.demo.linkeddatahub.com/") AS ?path)
    BIND (uri(concat(str($base), STRBEFORE(?path, "#"))) AS ?graph)
    BIND (uri(concat(str($base), ?path)) AS ?category)
}</code></pre>
                <p>When the import is complete, each category document carries its mapping. The Beverages document gains:</p>
                <pre>@prefix skos: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#&gt; .

&lt;https://localhost:4443/categories/1/#this&gt; a skos:Concept ;
    skos:closeMatch &lt;https://unesco-thesaurus.demo.linkeddatahub.com/concepts/concept3683/#this&gt; .</pre>
                <p>The mapped concept URIs stay remote on purpose: following one leads into the UNESCO Thesaurus dataspace through the
                    <a href="../../http-api/#ld-proxy">Linked Data proxy</a>, and queries can join the two dataspaces over their
                    <a href="../../http-api/#federation">federated</a> SPARQL endpoints.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="lead">If you are ready to import some RDF, see our step-by-step tutorial on <a href="../../../user-guide/import-data/import-rdf-data/">creating an RDF import</a>.</p>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral ;
        rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/#construct> .

<https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/#construct> dct:title "SPARQL 1.1 Query Language: CONSTRUCT" .